  {
   "kind": "paragraph",
   "text": "BY PETER LINDQVIST",
   "marks": [],
   "places": [
    {
     "kind": "page",
     "start": 0,
     "end": 18,
     "page": 1,
     "left": 0.062000000000000006,
     "top": 0.126,
     "width": 0.155,
     "height": 0.014999999999999986
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "kind": "paragraph",
   "text": "In the winter of 1987 a small team of archivists began to catalogue the papers of a provincial court that had been closed for almost a century. The boxes arrived in the back of a delivery van, tied with string and smelling of damp. Nobody expected much. Court records are mostly dull, and these were dull in the ordinary ways: wills, land disputes, complaints about fences. Then, in the fourth week, someone opened a ledger that did not belong. It held the daily weather, noted in a careful hand, for thirty-one years. The clerk who kept it had no instructions to do so. He had simply looked out of his window each morning and written down what he saw. Climate scientists later used his figures to test models of a century they had no other records for. The archivists, who had come for the lawsuits, found themselves arguing about rainfall.",
   "marks": [],
